==English==

===Etymology===
Originally {{m|en|buttonhold||a loop of string to hold a button down}}, but changed by [[folk etymology]] by influence of {{m|en|hole}}; see [[w:Folk_etymology|the Wikipedia article on folk etymology]]

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-us-buttonhole.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[hole]] through which a [[button]] is [[push]]ed to secure a garment or some part of one.
# {{lb|en|chiefly|British}} A [[flower]] worn in a buttonhole for decoration.
#: {{syn|en|boutonniere}}
# {{lb|en|medicine}} A small [[slot]]-like [[cut]] or [[incision]], made for example by an accident with the [[scalpel]].
#* '''2011''', George L. Spaeth, Helen Danesh-Meyer, Ivan Goldberg, ''Ophthalmic Surgery: Principles and Practice E-Book'' (page 220)
#*: The usual cause of conjunctival '''buttonholes''' is penetration of the tissue by the tip of a sharp instrument {{...}}

